Even though I've been playing for nearly ten years now (not counting
Hack 1.0.3), I've not actually ascended all classes. The trouble with
that kind of situation is that you end up suffering more and more as
you work down to the very hardest classes. After clearing Rogue I had
only WHT left to do; Wizard isn't really that hard even though I've
never previously done well with them, so I cleared that up pretty
quickly and contemplated the prospect of Healers and Tourists and
nothing else.

Kirsty the Herbalist         St:18/50 Dx:23 Co:18 In:23 Wi:23 Ch:18  Neutral
Astral Plane $:0  HP:183(183) Pw:39(246) AC:-35 Xp:20/5284623 T:75844

Well, now I've got nothing left but Tourists to play. I'm not a big
fan of the protection racket, but I think it makes sense with a Healer
- one particular conventionally approached game where I made the
utmost of all my resources and was still gradually ground down
convinced me. There was nothing wrong with my play there; the game was
just too hard. Incidentally, I don't always play gnomish healers; I allow
the game to select a random race for me.

Hence this Healer began by diving to the Mines. An altar on DL 2 was
noted for future reference, but there's little chain-saccing to be
done when you don't want to kill anything. I barely survived the
Mines; on what proved to be the level above the Town, I had to dig
down with a WoDig, breaking contact with my pet. Luckily I was able to
make it to the temple and donate 4 times, lowering my naked AC to 5. I
ran back upstairs and managed to hook up with my pet before finding
the beasties that had forced me to retreat. At this juncture I noticed
that my AC with elven mithril and all the other armour items the pet
had freed up was -5, so I decided to abandon spells for now and prod
beasties with an elven dagger.

I immediately returned to the altar and chain-sacced - after very
little effort, Hermes was forthcoming with Mjollnir, which certainly
made the decision to be a combat wombat a little easier to deal with.

I ground down to Sokoban on DL 8; in addition to the bag of holding, I
found two tins of spinach to complete the Sokoban workout, and a
glass orb. I established my primary stash in Sokoban - I would walk
between the altar on DL 2 and Sokoban a great many times before
descending to DL 9 and finding the altar there.

Now, back up to the Mines. A nymph was kind enough to yield a shield
of reflection, which finished off any prospect of spellcasting - I
would also find a helm of brilliance in the Mines, one of three total
I found, but this one was +0; and also +0 gauntlets of dexterity. All
this kit helped free up slots in the Castle wand later. My cat, Akane,
became an ice troll in a polytrap, and shortly afterwards I would
follow her. Becoming an ice troll trashed my armour, so I used Akane
to butcher dwarves for fresh equipment. I would destroy three complete
sets of dwarvish gear in polytraps while clearing the mines.

The real win in the Mines, however, was a magic lamp from a passing
ogre - I'd keep this for the entire game.

I took down two air elementals in the mines, but then found myself
transformed into an iron golem. Iron golems are pretty tough, but I
deliberately rubber-chickened myself into a stone golem only to
discover that this new form wasn't as powerful. The next round of
polytrap annoyance made Akane into a python and me into a winter wolf;
sadly Akane was too slow in this form, and I dumped her at the base of
the Mines.

I got the wine cellar ending, that remains my favourite - the six
potions of booze make for a good start on the holy water collection -
collected the luckstone and zoomed back up to the altar to sacrifice
some more. Werebane followed, which I wasn't overjoyed about, but then
Hermes gave out the Frost Brand.

Finally I ground down the main dungeon. I robbed a big shop on DL 10;
tamed a guardian naga hatchling (a terrible pet - it needed constant
healing and didn't grow up into a guardian naga before dying); had a
bigroom (and deliberately stripped off cloak and armour in order to
neutralise a polytrap before the monsters used it; nipped back up to
the mines to donate, and got two levels off an incubus; and then hit
the Quest.

I didn't much fancy tackling the Healer Nemesis straight off the bat,
but cleared out the first level. I'd hoped to get silver or gray
dragon scales, but didn't; however, even yellow DSM is better than
dwarvish mithril, so I made some of that. This let me use spells
effectively by popping my SoR on and off; fortunately nothing worse
than losing a few wands came of the times I forgot to put it back on.

Instead I continued down the dungeon. I sacrificed one snake and got
crowned; Vorpy joined my excessive weapons golfbag, now consisting of
Mojo, Frosty, Werebane, Vorpy, and eight elven daggers. My naked AC
reached 1, and as usual I stopped picking up gold. Somewhere I found
water walking boots, which certainly came in handy.

My first real woe came on DL 21, where a monster with a SoCM created a
demilich and a mind flayer at the same time. I killed a rubber chicken
and started blatting away, but ended up next to both of them and got
mindflayed anyway; this made for another diversion back up to DL 1, to
refresh my memory of the dungeon. The loss of object identities wasn't
too much of a nuisance, since I had the spell of identify, but I was
later forced to burn blank scrolls and marker charges on the 1/3
chance of writing enchant armour unknown - with a helm of brilliance
and GoDex, I really was wanting to improve my armour.

At Medusa I genocided L; I still had no magic resistance, and didn't
much like the idea of fumbling in my bag at the Castle while one of
the buff liches pounded on me. Another gray dragon failed to yield
scales here; Perseus gave me the shield (which I already had) and
boots of levitation, which I lugged about but never used.

At the Castle I went in the back way first, as I usually do - there's
no point in fighting all those nasties before checking the armour room
for goodies. This time a gray dragon yielded scales, and I did the
blank scroll shuffle mentioned above and made GDSM - as it turned out
I wouldn't use it for very long. I also found a CoDisplacement in the
armour room, which was certainly an improvement on my existing elven
cloak - I'd been granted stealth by Hermes, so there was no worry
there. 

I don't normally use conflict - I haven't since my 3.1.3 Valkyrie -
but I see that as more of a guideline, so I let the horde of nasties
in the Castle proper pound each other for a bit. Most of the game I'd
use rings of free action and +5 gain strength (more useful for a
gnome), but I resorted to conflict in the Castle, the Quest's final
level, and the Astral Plane.

The wand of wishing was 0:2 and I had not seen scrolls of charging
(otherwise I think it's better to write them than wish for them); I
trolled back up to my stash in Sokoban before using it up. On the way
back up, I found the portal to Fort Ludious on DL23, but since I had
little use for gold I left it well alone "for the time being" - as it
turned out I never cleared it out.

I preemptively genocided h, which may have been an overreaction as it
left me with no emergency genocides until I found one in the Valley
(and another in Vlad's).  I blew my five effective wishes on speed
boots, SDSM, a magic marker, a cloak of magic resistance, and a
spellbook of magic mapping. As luck would have it, I would find quite
a few of these things in the Quest; silver dragon scales (the dragon
was wearing them to begin with) and a magic marker.

I also found a second magic lamp on the Quest, and thought it might be
a good idea to wish up a spellbook of cure sickness; however, I hit
the "djinni vanishes" result. Since I now had a surplus of marker
charges, I blanked some junk spellbooks and shot for the 1/3 chance of
writing it - succeeding on the second attempt. As a result, as it
happens, I was never deathly sick from that point onwards.

I would find a WoCancel and a WoDeath midway down the Quest - the
former I carefully moved to my stash, and the latter would come in
handy. 

I arrived at the bottom of the Quest filled with fancy tactics
involving the ring of conflict, potions of paralysis and sickness,
etc. However as I was sneaking up on the Cyclops he woke up,
teleported to me, and yanked 2 levels out of me with the Staff, so I
just shot him with the wand of death, killing him. At this point I
switched to the Staff as my primary weapon and ditched Werebane into
my stash. The Staff is slow to kill undead and demons - but vampires
can't level drain you when you wield it, and demons can be dispatched
with Frosty or Mojo - and it made swift work of most other hostiles.

I started down into Gehennom, finding a SoGeno at the Valley. However
with magic mapping at 74% failure my mana was rapidly drained.  I
decided to return to my stash for my small supply of scrolls of magic
mapping, to use them when mana was low, but also to deviate from
standard procedure and prepare for the final run there and then,
rather than returning to my stash after finding the vibrating square
as I normally do.

I therefore left a heap of K-rations and scrolls of gold detection
(and my two crystal balls, which I eventually ditched) under a boulder
on DL 1, filled up with extra kit from the Sokoban stash, and left
food rations and extra holy water in the Elbereth-chest in the Castle
(I don't engrave Elbereth, but if someone wrote it already I'll use
it) - all was ready for my escape.

Vlad's AoLS was the first one I saw ever; I had no luck with his
throne, just as with every other throne. He also yielded a second
spare SoGeno.

The rest of Gehennom was easy. Neither Asmodeus nor Baalzebub made it
off their levels, and I managed to whip off Orcus's head with Vorpy
before he could empty the wand completely. My mana and scrolls
together saved me from exploring any mazes, but after I found the
Vibrating Square I had a bit of a puzzle, because I didn't want to
tackle Rodney without full mana. Luckily I found a magic trap in his
Tower and bounced on it until I got the magical explosion, which
recharged me. I gave him a quick zap with one of my WoDeaths, and
legged it down to the Sanctum.

I've said it before, but if you don't mess around clearing the
graveyard you can be in and out of the Sanctum like a rocket. In the
time it takes to clear the graveyard, a lot of monsters are generated
in the Western half of the level; as it was the aligned priests barely
slowed me down at all. I used my carefully saved WoSDD to find the
door quickly, killed the high priest in no time (the Staff works
amazingly well on spellcasting monsters), and ran for it - I didn't
have a cursed PoGL for a really stylish exit, but since I'd avoided
making noise to wake the graveyard I could leave out the way I came
in, pausing only to zap a second Rodney.

I had a lot of pain with the mysterious force at the bottom and the
top of Gehennom, but nothing in the middle; however, Rodney turned up
a third time on DL 43, getting another zap for his pains. He'd turn up
a fourth time on DL 15.

I required little of my cache at the Castle - for weight reasons I
couldn't carry any more water, but I blessed everything uncursed that
I didn't want to risk being cursed, and replenished my food supply.
Since rations are faster to eat than corpses, I live off them once
Rodney is dead.

I returned to my cache in Sokoban. Since it seemed unlikely I'd find
more gain energy potions, I blessed and cashed 5 extra healing and 7
healing for hitpoints. I'd already cashed 8 full healing for HP
earlier, and turned two into gain ability; I had 6 more full healing
with me for the last run. I did the usual stuff - replenished food and
water, blessed more stuff, refreshed my spells - then headed for the
surface.

Earth was a cakewalk as ever; after zapping Rodney here I had one zap
left. Air was more awkward; I donned a towel to keep an Archon from
bothering me so much, but then couldn't avoid the clouds, and in spite
of 283 HP I burned a PoFH. Halfway across the level I realised the
answer was to stop running and just smack the Archon, so I did that,
removed my towel, and made it out without further trouble. Fire was no
trouble; Water was fine until Rodney turned up, was missed by my last
death ray, and summoned a bunch of beasties - however, just then the
portal hove into view, so I dived through it and killed Rodney on the
Astral Plane instead.

With 283 max HP and 5 PoFH (and two AoLS) in reserve I wasn't too
worried about Death, so I tackled her altar (the middle one) first. It
was the wrong one; and in the process of getting in and back out,
Death cost me a good 100 of those max HP - I killed her twice, once
each way. Famine was next, on the left; however, I managed to dance
around him and never fought him at all. His altar was also wrong; on
the way out my guardian angel died, allowing me to use conflict, and I
also used a favourite new tactic of casting charm monster on the ants
and then displacing them to move rapidly - it helps if you kick down
the doors on the way in, to stop them from getting in your way on the
way out. I crawled back past Death, killing her a third time, and
prepared to tackle Pestilence - but to my delight he was messing
around in the circular corridor around the altar room, and I never had
to fight him at all.

